<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhang Bangchang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1081">1081</a>–<a href="/years/1127">1127</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhang Bangchang was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> chancellor installed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> Jin as emperor of the puppet state of Da Chu at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in 1127, after they captured the city and deported the Song emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Huizong</a> and Qinzong. He held the throne for about a month, then handed power back to the Song prince who became Emperor Gaozong, who later ordered him to commit suicide.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Zhang Bangchang, born in 1081, served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> as a prime minister. When the Jurchen Jin defeated the Song, they had neither expected nor wanted its fall: they had meant to weaken it in order to demand more tribute, they were busy strengthening their rule over the former lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, and the Song military outnumbered their own. Instead of pressing the invasion they adopted a strategy of using Chinese to control the Chinese, hoping that a proxy state could administer northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and collect the annual indemnity without Jurchen troops having to put down uprisings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1127 the Jin formed the buffer state of Chu and installed Zhang as its emperor. The offer was too attractive for him to resist, but he had reservations about the role, and he wore the emperor's formal clothing only when meeting Jin officials. Empress Dowager Yuanyou, who had been dismissed as empress by her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhezong">Emperor Zhezong</a>, was enlisted to strengthen the legitimacy of the puppet government.</p><p>Da Chu did not deter resistance in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Song commanders in towns scattered across the north kept their allegiance to the Song, and armed volunteers organised militias against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> presence, moved more by anger at Jurchen looting than by loyalty to the Song court. The insurgency hampered the ability of the Jin to control the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Da Chu was abolished when Zhang and Empress Dowager Yuanyou agreed to recognise Emperor Gaozong as ruler of the revived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a>. Zhang submitted to him, but on 1 November 1127 he was coerced into suicide. Gaozong gave the order under pressure from his chief councillor Li Gang, who opposed diplomatic reconciliation with the Jin and wanted Zhang executed for collaborating with them. The removal of Zhang and his buffer state breached the treaty the Jin and Song had negotiated, and the Jin resumed their war with the Song, an invasion hampered by Song loyalists in the north.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhang Bangchang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They forced <b>Zhang Bangchang</b>, a Song chief minister, to accept the throne as emperor of Chu.</blockquote><a href="/state/da-chu">Da Chu</a></li></ul></section>
